.TH SYS_DEBUG 2
.SH NAME
sys_debug \- print to the kernel log (SYS_DEBUG)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_debug(const char *s, size_t n);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_debug
prints the
.I n
bytes at
.I s
through the kernel's own output: the serial port, and the console's log
that
.BR sys_log (2)
and
.BR dmesg (1)
read. At most 199 bytes are taken per call; the rest is not printed. The
kernel writes each piece whole, under its lock, so lines of different
programs never mix.
.PP
Anyone may call it. The console server
.BR con (4)
sends its own output this way, so the serial wire shows what the screen
shows. Ordinary programs print with
.B printf
and
.BR con_write ,
which go to their terminal instead.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes printed (at most 199), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I s
is not readable memory of the caller.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_log (2),
.BR dmesg (1),
.BR con (4)
